# [Your Project Name]

**(Please replace '[Your Project Name]' with a relevant title and add a one or two-sentence description of your project here. For example: "A web application for managing tasks, built with Node.js and Express, and deployed on Vercel.")**

This project is a web application built with Node.js and Express, utilizing the EJS templating engine for server-side rendering. It features a modular routing system, cookie management, CORS support, and is configured for easy deployment on Vercel.

---

## Features

* **Backend Framework:** Built with Express.js, a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework.
* **Templating Engine:** Uses EJS (Embedded JavaScript templates) for dynamic HTML page generation.
* **Modular Routing:** Organizes routes into separate files for better maintainability (`routes/index.js`, `routes/Authentication.js`, `routes/adminRoute.mjs`).
* **Static File Serving:** Configured to serve static assets from the `uploads/` directory (see *Important Note on File Uploads* below).
* **Middleware:**
    * `cors`: Enables Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.
    * `express.urlencoded` & `express.json`: Parses incoming request bodies.
    * `cookie-parser`: Parses cookies attached to the client request object.
* **ES Modules:** Utilizes the modern JavaScript ES module system (`import`/`export`).
* **Vercel Ready:** Includes a `vercel.json` configuration file for seamless deployment to the Vercel platform.

---

## Important Note on File Uploads with Vercel

This project includes a static route `/uploads` configured with `express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'uploads'))`.

**Please be aware of the following when deploying to Vercel:**

* **Ephemeral Filesystem:** Vercel's serverless functions have an ephemeral (temporary) filesystem. This means any files written to the local filesystem (like user uploads to the `uploads/` directory at runtime) **will be lost** when the function scales down, restarts, or on new deployments.
* **Static Assets at Build Time:** Files that are part of your repository and included in the `uploads/` directory *at build time* will be served correctly.
* **Solution for Runtime Uploads:** If your application requires users to upload files that need to persist, you **must** use an external cloud storage service. Options include:
    * [Vercel Blob](https://vercel.com/storage/blob)
    * AWS S3
    * Google Cloud Storage
    * Cloudinary

You will need to modify your file upload logic to send files to one of these services and then serve them from their respective URLs.

